   "id": "665c52e8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "1. A model is linear when it follows the y=ax+b form. It takes input variables and ouputs a value that just has a constant rate of change.\n",
    "2. The dummy/one-hot-encoded variable is mainly used for categorical/qualitative variables that should be taken in as a numerical values. This can be done such that the model takes True (1) or False (0) variables.\n",
    "3. No because it usually outputs a numeric value. If doing a probability, it has to output from 0 to 1, which does not give any flexibility to put values into more than two categories. \n",
    "4. When there is high multicollinearity. When you test the model it has a high performance on training data but on testing and validation, it does badly.\n",
    "5. High multi-colinearity is when the variables in your model are too closely related and have a strong linear relationship. The two-stage least squares technique basically states that it will try to predict a variable that is too problematic for the model. This is done by first using other variables to estimate that problematic variable. In the second stage, the predicted version of that variable is used in the final model instead of the original one. This helps reduce the effect of the strong relationships between variables and makes the model more stable.\n",
    "6. You can incorporate nonlinear relationships by transformting the data such as doing the log/exponential/sqrt of the values. You could also add polynomials so that it makes the variables not a linear relationship and just make it more complex and easier to work with.\n",
    "7. There is a slope coefficient value increase/decrease in predicted value of y per every one unit increase in x.\n",
    "8. Train/test split and $k$-fold cross validation differ in how they divide and use the data. In a train/test split, the data is divided once into a training set and a testing set, and the model is trained and evaluated using that single split. In $k$-fold cross validation, the data is partitioned into $k$ equally sized sets (folds). The model is trained k times, each time using a different fold as the test set and the remaining folds as the training set. This allows the model to be evaluated on multiple splits of the data rather than just one.\n",
    "9.  It's chosen by the bias-variance trade-off of the cross-validation estimate. Higher k means less bias but more variance, but lower k means more bias and less variance. Pick your poison my dude."
   ]
  },
